cientist motivating them to be right at  the forefront of translation now what  about the women as I said before they're  really not part of the picture of the  scientists and their breakthrough babies  but they're still essential to  scientific innovation and translation in  the sphere of human reproduction so here  we need to think about who the women are  and what they do and here I think we can  that women lend themselves through the  kind of translational process by giving  these early adoption of technologies  like CRISPR a certain kind of maternal  of borodin and sorry Tracy my computer  yeah so the second group of people we  need to think about are women so women  are really central to the process of  scientific innovation and translation  today especially in the sphere of human  reproduction they're still necessary  because we haven't yet got past  pregnancy and they're essential because  it seems to me that the that the  involvement of women in this process  actually lends some legitimation and  authority to it so we can say that  there's a kind of maternal Authority in  the fact that a woman house herself  agreed to use this technology to have  her children now interestingly an  anthropologist by the name of Reina wrap  came up with the idea of Moral  pioneering in reproduction some years  ago in the context of thinking about  prenatal testing technologies but I  think we can see that this concept is  really pertinent here because women who  are using these technologies are really  right at the forefront of the kind of  moral debates of whether these  technologies are acceptable or not so  they're very much kind of moral pioneers  in terms of the translation of  innovative technologies so it seems  clear to me that women are actually part  of the legitimation process for  innovative technologies but here I want  to be clear that women are neither  hoodwinked nor intentionally lending  themselves to legitimate these  technologies instead I would say  using a quote from the French  philosopher Michel Foucault that people  know what they do frequently they even  know why they do what they do but what  they don't know is what what they do  does and that's a very cute way of  saying that people don't know what the  consequences of their actions may be or  what the effects of their actions may be  so the last point that I want to make  about women is that maternal and  parental desire itself acts as a driver  of innovation so we couldn't say this  because scientists often point to the  importance of reproduction and the  strength of that maternal or parental  desire in terms of legitimating their  use of technology but we also need to  know that the that desire itself is not  straightforward because the desire and  the technology are themselves  co-produced the desire is not  pre-existent to the technology nobody's  sitting around 10 years ago saying I'd  really like to genome edit my child it's  only when the technology becomes  possible that the desire of cell becomes  pertinent and actually starts to drive  that that process of translation so what  about the children who are they and how  do they live so interestingly when we  talk about the children of innovative  technologies there are two things that  we need to keep in mind on the one hand  what we're talking about is the figure  of the future child the child not yet in  existence but who might come into the  existence if we are able to use these  technologies so in that sense the child  is a kind of prolific target of  responsibility they're a target of  responsibility that does not yet exist  but we should also keep in mind that we  are talking about actual children that  will exist or do exist so if we think  about the history of IVF for instance we  think about the children who have been  born of that technology and in  particular the first child born of that  technology  what is it like then to be a  breakthrough baby what kind of life does  one lead as a breakthrough baby now  Louise Brown the child born the first  child born of IVF often kind of  downplays the importance of or the  effect of being a breakthrough baby on  her life but we might note that not  everyone has their 40th birthday at the  British Museum and has it broadcast in  the international press so there's  clearly some difference to the lives of  breakthrough babies there might also be  different moral responsibilities that  the children of innovation have by  virtue of their experimental black birth  would they for instance be obliged to  submit to ongoing medical testing to  making their lives public and so on  because that's how we will know if these  technologies are actually safe or not we  can't prove we can suggest that perhaps  they have an obligation to not reproduce  because really that's the only way that  we will be sure that the technology is  safe  we won't know whether it's safe for the  next generation or not until that next  generation comes along so we might also  say then that there in fact obliged to  reproduce because that's the only way  that we will know that the technology is  safe so it may be that the children of  experimental birth actually have certain  kinds of responsibilities or obligations  by virtue of that birth now the last  thing I want to talk about are the  technologies themselves what do the  technologies themselves actually do in  the process of translation well there  are a few things to say but the first of  these is that technologies make possible  other other technologies that are  neither intended nor foreseen so if we  think about in vitro fertilization for  instance no one imagined when that was  being developed that it would give rise  to other technologies such as  pre-implantation genetic diagnosis that  was just not part of the picture  and yet PGD is only possible because i  it is possible we can also say that  technologies have social effects that  may not be foreseen or intended so using  IVF it's an example again no one foresaw  that I've yet would give rise to a  system of international commercial  surrogacy or could give rise and yet  that's what it's done it's made possible  certain kinds of international  commercial surrogacy arrangements so  what also comes from that is that  technologies make some forms of social  existence possible and put others into  question so when we think about IVF  again and gamete donation that process  of IVF and the possibility of using  donated gametes has really put into  question the importance of genetic  relatedness in a way that four years ago  just wasn't questioned at all now I'm  not saying that  prior to IVF all families were based on  genetic relatedness that that's clearly  not true but the way in which we thought  about genetic relatedness was very  different from how we think about it now  so given those things we think the  question to ask is what might genome  editing doing what kinds of other  technologies might it give rise to what  social effects may enhance and what  forms of social existence may it make  possible and what forms of social  existence may render impossible in other  words what are the opportunity costs of  using CRISPR case nine because  everything has opportunity costs any  Avenue of action will have an  opportunity cost so what are the  opportunity cost of using CRISPR cash  nine from the germline genome editing so  this takes me back to my concluding  slide which is and returning us to the  question of ethical features so I want  to emphasize that the point that I've  made is that the context of translation  in human reproduction is very  multi-dimensional there are a lot of  different pressures coming into play  there are a lot of different actors and  actors with different effects and  implications and power and so on I  haven't given an exhaustive list of what  those actors are here I've just given a  small selection of what they might think  we also might think about institutional  review boards for instance what effect  do they have what do they do within the  process of translation but I won't again  push home my central point that  responsibility associated with  innovation is necessarily responsibility  for the future and helps to create in  the process of taking up any particular  technology or using a particular  particular technology we create certain  futures and render others impossible and  this reminds us of the centrality of  intersubjectivity  and vulnerability in the process of  translation translation is about a  shared world as much as it is about  individual desires and interests so in  relation to genome editing I think we  must think what it is that we are doing  that is what world are we creating for  future generations not just for us but  for our children and our children's  children thank you
 

